15 0 obj
<< /A 47 0 R /Alt (Diagram illustrating engineered T-cells using DARPins for targeted cancer cell recognition. Panel A shows DARPins binding to cancer cells, highlighting their stability, signaling reduction, and adaptability for logic circuits. Panel B depicts engineered T-cell activation via antigen recognition; activation requires correct tumor context, reducing off-tumor toxicity.) /K 0 /P 8 0 R /Pg 32 0 R /S /Figure /T () >>
endobj
16 0 obj
<< /A 48 0 R /Alt (Diagram comparing three CAR T-cell strategies against cancer cells. A\) Tandem CAR uses scFv anti-CD19 and anti-CD20, triggering degranulation and cytokine production. B\) Dual CAR utilizes scFv anti-CD19 and anti-BCMA, leading to tumor lysis and reduced antigen escape. C\) Nanobody-based CAR incorporates scFv anti-CD47 and nanobody anti-PD-L1, modulating the tumor microenvironment and engaging tumor-infiltrating lymphocytes. Each approach shows interaction with cancer cells, resulting in cell death.) /K 0 /P 8 0 R /Pg 33 0 R /S /Figure /T () >>
endobj
17 0 obj
<< /A 49 0 R /Alt <> /K 0 /P 8 0 R /Pg 35 0 R /S /Figure /T () >>
endobj
18 0 obj
<< /A 50 0 R /Alt (Diagram illustrating a biological process and clinical trial design. Panel A shows logic-gated gene expression in epithelial cells with HLA-A*02 and EGFR. Panel B depicts active CAR targeting tumoral cells, releasing granzymes. Panel C displays reduced MHC-I expression with shRNA anti-B2M for GvHD reduction. Panel D describes Trial DENALI-1 as a phase 1/2 study to assess safety, tolerability, and response rate, represented by human figures.) /K 0 /P 8 0 R /Pg 36 0 R /S /Figure /T () >>
endobj
